.he "cmp (1)                      8/16/26                      cmp (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
cmp
- compare two files
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
cmp [ -ls ] file1 file2 [ skip1 [ skip2 ] ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Cmp compares two files and says nothing if they are the same.  If they
differ, it reports the byte and line number of the first difference;
if one file is an initial piece of the other, it says so.  Where file1
is -, the standard input is used.
.sp
The exit code is 0 for identical files, 1 for different files, and 2
for trouble such as a missing file.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-l	Print every difference, as a decimal byte number and the two bytes in octal.

.ti -10
-s	Print nothing; the exit code alone tells whether the files differ.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
The skip arguments, which the Berkeley man page left out though the
program has always taken them, tell cmp to start that many bytes into
each file.  A number with a leading 0 is octal; otherwise it is
decimal.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
diff (1)
